course: dictionary
lesson: 13867
title: Side by side
level: primary-5
stars: 2
explain: Juxtaposed means placed side by side, especially so that you can see how different two things are. Juxtapose is the verb, so you juxtapose two things when you put them next to each other. Juxtaposes and juxtaposing are other forms of the same word.
explain: «The painter juxtaposed a bright yellow sun with a dark, stormy sky.» The yellow sun and the black sky sit next to each other, so the sun looks even brighter. The painter put them together on purpose, to show how different they are.
explain: Look for the clues. Juxtaposed is often followed by with or against: «The tiny cottage was juxtaposed against a huge glass tower.» The word tells you that two things are being compared by being placed together.
fact: Juxtaposed means placed side by side.
fact: Juxtapose is the verb: to juxtapose two things is to put them next to each other.
fact: Writers and artists juxtapose things to show how different they are.
fact: «Juxtaposed» is a formal word, so you will mostly meet it in art, English and exams.
fact: Common phrases are «juxtaposed with» and «juxtaposed against».
exercise: «The painter juxtaposed a bright yellow sun with a dark, stormy sky.» What does juxtaposed mean here? | Painted over with a cloud | Made smaller than the sky | Hidden behind a hill | *Placed side by side to show how different they are
exercise: «The poem juxtaposes happy and sad words.» What is the poet doing with the happy and sad words? | Reading them aloud slowly | Sorting them by length | *Placing them side by side | Losing them
exercise: «The small cottage was juxtaposed ___ a huge glass tower.» Which word fills the gap? | behind | *with | upwards | under
test: Which sentence uses juxtaposed correctly? | The artist juxtaposed her lunch at midnight. | The artist juxtaposed a sleepy cat. | The artist juxtaposed slowly under the table. | *The artist juxtaposed a red circle and a blue square on the same page.
test: Yesterday the writer ___ hot and cold in one line. Which form fits the gap? | juxtapose | *juxtaposed | juxtaposing | juxtaposes
exam: Why does a poet juxtapose light and dark in a poem? | To make the poem longer | To show the poet is tired | *To show how different light and dark are, and make each one stand out | To tell you what the weather is like
